  -
    title: Introduction
    start_index: 2
    end_index: 2
    nodes:
      [2,]{title,start_index,end_index,summary,split_document_anchor,chunk_excerpt_index,node_id}:
        Recurrent Neural Networks and Attention Mechanisms,2,2,"This section elaborates on the use of recurrent neural networks and attention mechanisms in sequence modeling and transduction problems, emphasizing the limitations of sequential computation and the benefits of attention mechanisms.","Recurrent neural networks, long short-term memory [ 13] and gated recurrent [ 7] neural networks",0,0002
        Attention Mechanisms in Sequence Modeling,2,2,"The section discusses the role of attention mechanisms in sequence modeling and transduction models, explaining how they allow modeling dependencies without regard to their distance in the input or output sequences.","Attention mechanisms have become an integral part of compelling sequence modeling and transduc- tion models in various tasks, allowing modeling of dependencies without regard to their distance in the input or output sequences [ 2,19].",0,0003
    summary: "The introduction discusses the importance of recurrent neural networks and attention mechanisms in sequence modeling and transduction problems, highlighting the limitations of sequential computation and the potential of attention mechanisms."
    split_document_anchor: 1 Introduction
    chunk_excerpt_index: 0
    node_id: 0001
  -
    title: Background
    start_index: 2
    end_index: 2
    nodes:
      [2,]{title,start_index,end_index,summary,split_document_anchor,chunk_excerpt_index,node_id}:
        Reducing Sequential Computation,2,2,"This section describes efforts to reduce sequential computation in sequence transduction models, including the use of convolutional neural networks and attention mechanisms.","The goal of reducing sequential computation also forms the foundation of the Extended Neural GPU [16], ByteNet [ 18] and ConvS2S [ 9], all of which use convolutional neural networks as basic building block, computing hidden representations in parallel for all input and output positions.",0,0005
        Transformer Model,2,2,"The section introduces the Transformer model, which relies entirely on an attention mechanism to draw global dependencies between input and output, allowing for significantly more parallelization.","In this work we propose the Transformer, a model architecture eschewing recurrence and instead relying entirely on an attention mechanism to draw global dependencies between input and output.",0,0006
    summary: "The background section provides an overview of recent work on reducing sequential computation in sequence transduction models, including the Extended Neural GPU, ByteNet, and ConvS2S, and introduces the Transformer model."
    split_document_anchor: 2 Background
    chunk_excerpt_index: 0
    node_id: 0004
  -
    title: Model Architecture
    start_index: 2
    end_index: 3
    nodes:
      -
        title: Encoder and Decoder Stacks
        start_index: 3
        end_index: 3
        summary: "This section details the architecture of the encoder and decoder stacks in the Transformer, including the use of multi-head self-attention and feed-forward networks."
        split_document_anchor: 3.1 Encoder and Decoder Stacks
        chunk_excerpt_index: 0
        node_id: 0008
      -
        title: Attention
        start_index: 3
        end_index: 4
        nodes:
          [2,]{title,start_index,end_index,summary,split_document_anchor,chunk_excerpt_index,node_id}:
            Scaled Dot-Product Attention,4,4,"This subsection describes the scaled dot-product attention mechanism, which is used in the Transformer to compute the compatibility function between queries and keys.",3.2.1 Scaled Dot-Product Attention,0,0010
            Multi-Head Attention,4,5,The multi-head attention section explains how the Transformer uses multiple attention heads to jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions.,3.2.2 Multi-Head Attention,0,0011
        summary: "The attention section explains the concept of attention in the Transformer, including scaled dot-product attention and multi-head attention."
        split_document_anchor: 3.2 Attention
        chunk_excerpt_index: 0
        node_id: 0009
      -
        title: "Position-wise Feed-Forward Networks"
        start_index: 5
        end_index: 5
        summary: "This section describes the position-wise feed-forward networks used in the Transformer, which are applied to each position separately and identically."
        split_document_anchor: "3.3 Position-wise Feed-Forward Networks"
        chunk_excerpt_index: 0
        node_id: 0012
      -
        title: Embeddings and Softmax
        start_index: 5
        end_index: 6
        summary: "The embeddings and softmax section explains how the Transformer uses learned embeddings to convert input and output tokens to vectors of dimension dmodel, and how the decoder output is converted to predicted next-token probabilities."
        split_document_anchor: 3.4 Embeddings and Softmax
        chunk_excerpt_index: 0
        node_id: 0013
      -
        title: Positional Encoding
        start_index: 6
        end_index: 6
        summary: The positional encoding section describes how positional encodings are added to the input embeddings to inject information about the relative or absolute position of tokens in the sequence.
        split_document_anchor: 3.5 Positional Encoding
        chunk_excerpt_index: 0
        node_id: 0014
    summary: "The model architecture section describes the architecture of the Transformer, including the encoder and decoder stacks, and the use of self-attention and feed-forward networks."
